===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|seisin}}, {{m|enm|seysen}}, from {{der|en|fro|seisin}}, from the verb {{m|fro|seisir}}, from {{der|en|VL.|*saciō}}, from the same {{der|en|ine-pro|-}} root as {{cog|got|𐍃𐌰𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽}} and {{cog|ang|settan}}. More at {{l|en|seize}}, {{l|en|seise}}.

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|legal|common law|historical}} An [[entitlement]] to a [[freehold]] estate with a right to immediate possession; dates from [[feudal]] times but is still used in technical discussions of real property law today.
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} The act of taking possession.
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} The thing possessed; [[property]].
#* {{RQ:Hale Common Law}}
#*:Disseisin is a large title, and is an unlawful ENTRY AND OUSTER of him that has an ACTUAL '''seisin''' and freehold

